<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Early Netherlandish painting</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th–16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Flemish Primitives</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Early Netherlandish painting is the work of painters active in the Burgundian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in the fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, often called the Flemish Primitives. Working mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, artists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-eyck">Jan van Eyck</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogier-van-der-weyden">Rogier van der Weyden</a>, Hugo van der Goes and Hans Memling perfected oil painting and minutely detailed realism. Their altarpieces and portraits influenced painting across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/early-netherlandish-painting.jpg" alt="Early Netherlandish painting" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan van Eyck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Van_Eyck_-_Arnolfini_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The school begins roughly with Robert Campin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-eyck">Jan van Eyck</a> in the 1420s and lasts at least until the death of Gerard David in 1523, though many scholars extend it to the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> in 1566 or 1568. It flourished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, Mechelen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, in the lands of the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a>. Its roots lie in late Gothic manuscript illumination, which after 1380 gained new realism and skill, peaking with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limbourg">Limbourg</a> brothers and an artist known as Hand G, who worked on the Turin-Milan Hours around 1420 and is thought to have been either Jan van Eyck or his brother Hubert.</p><p>The rise of Burgundian power made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> the political and economic centre of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and a merchant and banker class demanded art. Painters sold through workshops and market stalls to foreign princes and merchants, under rules set by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> that limited the number of masters in each city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Campin, van Eyck and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogier-van-der-weyden">Rogier van der Weyden</a> made naturalism the dominant style of fifteenth-century northern painting. They studied light, shadow and reflection, replaced flat perspective with three-dimensional space and showed people with a greater range of emotion. In the Arnolfini Portrait van Eyck arranged the scene as if the viewer had just entered the room. Other major painters include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dieric-bouts">Dieric Bouts</a>, Petrus Christus, Hans Memling, Hugo van der Goes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieronymus-bosch">Hieronymus Bosch</a>.</p><p>Most works are oils on panel, either single panels or diptychs, triptychs and polyptychs; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent-altarpiece">Ghent Altarpiece</a>, completed in 1432, had different arrangements for weekdays, Sundays and holidays. Subjects are mostly religious scenes and small portraits, full of symbolism, and scenes from the life of Mary predominate. Secular portraits hardly existed before 1430, and van Eyck's Léal Souvenir of 1432 is one of the earliest surviving. Landscape stayed a background detail until the mid-1460s. The region was also known for tapestry, illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, stained glass and carved retables.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Italian ideals reached northern painting from the 1490s, as more Netherlandish painters travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Most early works were destroyed in waves of iconoclasm, notably the Beeldenstorm of 1566, and only a few thousand survive. The art lost favour from the early seventeenth century to the mid-nineteenth, and scholars needed almost a century more to settle attributions, so some major painters' lives are known only in outline. Max J. Friedländer's work in the 1920s and Erwin Panofsky's in the 1950s and 1960s made the field a major subject of art history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Early Netherlandish painting” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the dukes of Burgundy, Flanders became the core of a rich new state, and its painters, the <b>Flemish Primitives</b>, transformed European art; Jan van Eyck worked in Bruges, and the Ghent Altarpiece was completed in 1432.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
